+# Narrow the Veterinarian-Search Non-Goal to Admit a Specialty Filter
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope, leaving only owners searchable. The demonstration framing (G-1) justified that: owner search already teaches paged prefix search once, and a second search surface elsewhere adds breadth without teaching a new pattern.
+
+A request has since arrived to narrow only the veterinarian portion. Two distinct capabilities hide under "searching for a veterinarian." One is free-text search over veterinarian records — the same paged-prefix pattern owner search already demonstrates. The other is filtering the existing directory by an attribute it already shows the reader. They differ in what they teach and in what they cost.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Keep NG-9 whole** — no veterinarian filtering of any kind. Preserves the original boundary but declines a filter that reuses data already on the page.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely** — allow veterinarian search of every kind, including free-text. Adds a second full search surface that only repeats owner search.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** — admit filtering the directory by a displayed specialty; keep free-text veterinarian search out.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays is in scope (REQ-VET-003). Free-text veterinarian search stays a non-goal under NG-9. The line is whether the capability introduces a new search surface (out) or narrows an existing directory by data it already presents (in).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The veterinarian directory gains a specialty filter on both its page and its machine-readable surface, reusing an attribute already displayed — no new search surface.
+- Free-text veterinarian search remains out of scope; NG-9 still declines it.
+- The machine-readable surface at the second route, formerly an unconsumed artifact recorded as a defect under REQ-VET-001, becomes a supported surface carrying REQ-VET-003. REQ-VET-002 stays withdrawn on the Superseded list; its ID is not reused.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md#req-vet-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003)
